Subject: the inode allocator moves on from a group whose bitmap is full

ext4_ialloc_alloc_inode walks the block groups looking for a free inode.
When a group's descriptor says it has free inodes but its bitmap has no
clear bit, the ENOSPC branch released the group and did `continue` -- which
skipped the ++bgid at the bottom of the loop. Same group, re-read, its
checksum re-verified, its bitmap re-scanned, forever: an infinite loop from
a create on a read-write mount, holding the mount.

Found by the CI fuzz smoke on the first run that built the harness on a
runner: a 5 MiB read-write mutant timed out at 20 s there, and here the
same input ran a `create` past a 60 s deadline. A descriptor and a bitmap
that disagree is corruption on the medium, and the counters are e2fsck's to
fix; the allocator's job is to move to the next group. It does now, and the
same create completes in a second. Fixture 0013 pins it.

--- a/src/ext4_ialloc.c
+++ b/src/ext4_ialloc.c
@@ -328,6 +328,18 @@
 				if (rc != EOK)
 					return rc;
 
+				/*
+				 * The descriptor said this group had free inodes and
+				 * the bitmap says it has none. That is corruption on
+				 * the medium, and it used to be an infinite loop: this
+				 * continue skipped the ++bgid at the bottom, so the
+				 * same group was re-read, its checksum re-verified and
+				 * its bitmap re-scanned forever, from a create on a
+				 * read-write mount. Found by the CI fuzz smoke on its
+				 * first successful run. Move on to the next group; the
+				 * counters are wrong and e2fsck will say so.
+				 */
+				++bgid;
 				continue;
 			}
 
